# ── Chapter 9 fidelity pins: "The Vote Conviction Does Not Take" ─────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/09-the-vote-conviction-does-not-take.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 24

# "Hano is in custody. Hano still has the right to vote."
? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

? travel(Hano).
# => FALSE

? decide(Hano, Ballot).
# => TRUE

# "the franchise follows for Hano exactly as it follows for anyone"
? decide(Jala, Ballot).
# => TRUE

# "Movement, and nothing else." — checked item by item
? person(Hano).
# => TRUE

? expresses(Hano).
# => TRUE

? false(Hano).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, Hano).
# => FALSE

# "The harsher test" — voiding does not reach the franchise either. Bela is
# voided and remains enfranchised. The pair must sit adjacent: the TRUE
# franchise conclusion only carries the section's claim while the voiding
# verdict stands beside it.
? false(Bela).
# => TRUE

? decide(Bela, Ballot).
# => TRUE

# Cira is a person and the supplied record lacks typed evidence that the common
# adulthood threshold was reached. The FALSE results establish only the bounded
# executable answer: they do not establish Cira's outside age or legal status.
? person(Cira).
# => TRUE

? prevents(Cira, GeneralAdulthoodDenial).
# => FALSE

? decide(Cira, Ballot).
# => FALSE

# "The vote does not have that protection." — felon disenfranchisement COMPILES.
# This is the chapter's honest half. If a later revision armours the franchise,
# this pin flips to a refusal and the closing sections must be rewritten.
:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) & match($x, GeneralAdult) & ~prisoner($x) -> decide($x, Ballot).

# ── MISSING EVIDENCE LOOKS LIKE NOT YET. Ground facts below persist ──────────
# Esa teaches, was examined, and is somebody's recorded child. None proves an
# age. Without typed adulthood evidence the bounded model derives neither the
# anti-denial wall nor the franchise conclusion. Supplying that evidence makes
# both derive; it does not constitute adulthood or prove an outside clock. The
# pair is self-controlling: FALSE holds before the supplied evidence and TRUE
# after it.
? prevents(Esa, GeneralAdulthoodDenial).
# => FALSE

? decide(Esa, Ballot).
# => FALSE

at(Esa, GeneralAdulthood).

? prevents(Esa, GeneralAdulthoodDenial).
# => TRUE

? decide(Esa, Ballot).
# => TRUE

# [v0.9 2026-08-04] D1: keep the exact-name admission resident while refusing
# the forged franchise conclusion. This block sits immediately before the
# mandatory-final resident rule so it widens no unrelated pin context. The
# mixed assertion must roll back its otherwise legal person fact atomically,
# and the last refusal holds the corpus's three-place shape beside Hano's live
# two-place derivation above.
:accept
admits("decide").

:refuse reasoning /`decide` is declared derived-only/
person(Vote_Probe) & decide(Vote_Probe, Ballot).

? person(Vote_Probe).
# => FALSE

:refuse reasoning /`decide` is declared derived-only/
decide(Ghost, Ballot, Election).

# ── "AND THEN NOTHING HAPPENS." MUST BE LAST, AND MUST BE UNSCOPED ───────────
# The scoped accept above proves the clause LOADS and then puts the base back —
# which is exactly why it cannot prove the chapter's corrected sentence. This
# one leaves the clause RESIDENT, and the query underneath is the sentence:
# with the standard disenfranchisement formula standing in force, the convicted
# man still holds the franchise, because this record only ever adds and the
# wider franchise rule is still there saying so.
#
# DO NOT EXPECT THE QUERY TO DEPEND ON THE ACCEPT. Measured both ways: drop the
# accept and the verdict is identical, because the franchise conclusion was
# always the wider rule's doing. That identity IS the pinned claim — the clause is in
# force and changes no answer — the same shape as the no-dead-conjuncts
# fixture, where nothing moving is the result. What this block adds over the
# scoped accept above is the word RESIDENT: the hostile clause is not merely
# loadable, it is standing in the record while the answer stays. An accepted
# rule stays in the KB, so nothing may be pinned after this block.
:accept
all $x: person($x) & match($x, GeneralAdult) & ~prisoner($x) -> decide($x, Ballot).

? decide(Hano, Ballot).
# => TRUE
